# Line endings are a checkout-time decision, and the default on Windows is to
# make one that breaks this repository.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is Git's Windows default, and it rewrites text files to
# CRLF as they land in the working tree. The committed blobs stay LF — that part
# was verified twice and is not the problem. The problem is the shebang: a
# checkout of `scripts/start-local-poc.sh` becomes `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and
# the kernel then looks for an interpreter literally named `bash\r`:
#
#   /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# So `npm run poc` — the one command the setup instructions give — fails on a
# Windows clone driven from WSL, which is the exact configuration this project
# is developed and demonstrated on.
#
# Pinning the working-tree ending here overrides the user's autocrlf setting for
# this repository, so the checkout is correct whatever the reviewer's machine is
# configured to do.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files where a stray CR is fatal rather than untidy.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf

# Never touch bytes that are not text.
*.jpg binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
